<SiteLayout title="Webhook Inbox & Replay" eyebrow="Project 03">
  <section class="page-hero compact"><div class="kicker">Developer tooling / Event delivery / Reliability</div><h1>Understand failures.<br /><em>Replay with care.</em></h1><p class="lead">A self-hosted webhook inbox for inspecting incoming events and reproducing integration problems without guessing.</p></section>
  <p class="pill">On the roadmap</p><div class="interlude"></div>
  <div class="page-content prose"><h2>The problem</h2><p>Webhooks are deceptively simple until a destination times out, a retry arrives twice, or a malformed event causes repeated failures. Reproducing the behavior often requires more work than implementing the original integration.</p><h2>Planned core features</h2><ul><li>A configurable endpoint that captures incoming webhook requests.</li><li>An inspectable delivery log with status, timestamps, and redacted payloads.</li><li>Safe manual replay and controlled automatic retries with backoff.</li><li>Delivery attempts and a clear, searchable failure history.</li><li>Local Docker setup and synthetic sample events.</li></ul><h2>Engineering questions to explore</h2><p>How do we preserve event ordering when it matters? How can a consumer behave idempotently under at-least-once delivery? What belongs inside a database transaction, and what should happen asynchronously? Where do dead-letter queues and Kafka help—and where would they be excessive?</p><h2>Security matters here</h2><p>The first public release should restrict outbound destinations to avoid server-side request forgery, authenticate administrative actions, redact secrets, limit payload sizes, and prevent unbounded retries. Those protections are part of the product rather than afterthoughts.</p><div class="notice">This page is a roadmap. It does not claim the tool or its security measures are implemented yet.</div><p><a class="text-link" href="/projects/">← Back to projects</a></p></div>
</SiteLayout>
